<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cachar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cachar is a district in the Barak valley of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, with its headquarters at Silchar. In the 18th century the Dimasa kings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a> moved their capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaspur">Khaspur</a> in its plains, and the region took its name from them. After the last king died in 1830, the British annexed the plains of Cachar in 1832.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cachar.jpg" alt="Cachar" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sandydessert · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ruins_of_Kachari_kingdom.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cachar district occupies 3,786 square kilometres in the Barak valley of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. It is bounded on the south by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mizoram">Mizoram</a>, on the east by Manipur and on the north by the Barail and Jayantia hill ranges. The Barak River is its main river, with tributaries including the Jiri and Jatinga from the north and the Sonai and Daleswari from the south. The district is mostly plains with several hill ranges, and the wetlands sometimes flood and form lakes. It is mostly tropical evergreen forest, with large tracts of rainforest in the north and south, and its annual rainfall exceeds 3,000 millimetres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> a conflict between two Dimasa Kachari princes led the younger, Dakhin, to build a new capital in the Barak valley, and his followers became known as the Twipra Kingdom. In 1562 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a> of the Koch dynasty captured the valley and gave his brother Kamal Narayan authority over it. His descendants ruled until the 18th century as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaspur">Khaspur</a> kingdom. When the last Koch ruler, Bhima Singha, died without sons, the Dimasas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maibang">Maibang</a> moved to Khaspur, through his daughter Kanchani's marriage to the Dimasa prince Laxmichandra, and the two kingdoms merged as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a>.</p><p>The British moved into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> in 1824 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a>. They sent the army officer T. Fisher to oversee the region and annexed the plains of Cachar on 14 August 1832, justifying the step by the doctrine of lapse. Northern Cachar remained a separate principality until the British occupied it in 1854, citing the misconduct of its rulers. Cachar became part of the Chief Commissionership of Assam on 6 February 1874. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/partition-of-india">partition of India</a> in 1947 the Karimganj sub-division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sylhet">Sylhet</a> joined the district, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dima-hasao">Dima Hasao</a>, Karimganj and Hailakandi were separated from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Silchar is the district headquarters. At the 2011 census Cachar had 1,736,617 inhabitants; Bengali is spoken by about three quarters of them, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> is the main religion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">Rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> are the main crops. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khaspur">Khaspur</a>, 20 kilometres from Silchar, the Sun Gate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lion-gate">Lion Gate</a>, the king's palace and a temple survive from the 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimasa-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a>. Barail Wildlife Sanctuary, the only one in the district, was established in 2004.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cachar district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Repeated wars with the Ahom kingdom, which sacked Dimapur in 1536, pushed the kings south to Maibang in the North Cachar hills, and in the 18th century to Khaspur in the plains of <b>Cachar</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kachari-kingdom">Kachari kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
